  "worked_solution": "From the equation of the line $l$, we know that it passes through the point $A(2,3,-4)$ and it is parallel to the vector $v=\u27e8-3,2,-1\u27e9.$ Therefore, its parametric equations are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\u27e82,3,-4\u27e9+t\u27e8-3,2,-1\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e82-3t,3+2t,-4-t\u27e9,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nLet's also write the equation of the plane in the dot product form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x-2y+z-38 & =0 \\\\ 3x-2y+z & =38 \\\\ \u27e8x,y,z\u27e9\\cdot \u27e83,-2,1\u27e9 & =38 \\\\ r\\cdot \u27e83,-2,1\u27e9 & =38\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $r=\u27e82-3t,3+2t,-4-t\u27e9$ into the equation of the plane, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r\\cdot \u27e83,-2,1\u27e9 & =38 \\\\ \u27e82-3t,3+2t,-4-t\u27e9\\cdot \u27e83,-2,1\u27e9 & =38 \\\\ 3(2-3t)-2(3+2t)+(-4-t) & =38 \\\\ 6-9t-6-4t-4-t & =38 \\\\ -14t & =42 \\\\ t & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to get the position vector $p$ of the intersection point $P,$ we substitute $t=-3$ into the vector equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p & =\u27e82-3(-3),3+2(-3),-4-(-3)\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e811,-3,-1\u27e9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we get $P(11,-3,-1).$",
